'''Shas'O Vior'la Shovah Kais Mont'yr''', more commonly known as '''O'Shovah''' or '''Commander Farsight''', is the leader of the [[Farsight Enclaves]], having lived for at least three centuries, considerably beyond the ordinary lifespan of [[T'au]] [[Fire Caste]] members. The breakaway Farsight Enclave maintains a strongly martial tradition closely based on the [[Vior'la Sept]], O'Shovah's birthplace.{{Fn|4c}}

===History===
====Early Life and Career====
Even before he was known as Shovah, the young [[Vior'la]]n absorbed and memorised every facet of the Vior'la [[Fire Warrior]] training academy. He spent much time in the '''Mont'yr Battle Dome''' war simulations and quickly became able to memorise the [[Code of Fire]] from end to end. He even deciphered the combat codes and signals used by his superiors, taking advantage of his knowledge to monitor their communiques. Despite being several years younger than most T'au within the Mont'yr Battle Dome, Shoh quickly outperformed his classmates. During Shoh's graduation from the academy, the legendary [[Commander Puretide]] was there to inspect the ceremony.{{Fn|4c}}

Immediately after graduation and induction as a [[Shas'la]], Shoh and his graduating class were deployed to a warzone, combating the [[Arachen]] incursion of the [[Western Veil]]. Shas'la Shoh made a great impact on the spirit of the Fire Caste troops stationed in the Veil, and was closely observed by his old teachers with mixed opinions; some saw great potential in the young warrior, but other former instructors had become disgusted with his arrogance. As a result, instructors such as [[Sha'kan'thas]] made sure Shoh was assigned on the front line in every deployment on the Western veil. If he was a prodigy born for glory he would rise to the challenge, if not then a troublesome student would be out of the way. Despite these challenges, Shoh preformed well against the Arachen for the next four years as a Shas'la trooper.{{Fn|4d}}

By the end of his tenure as a Fire Warrior, it was Shoh's suggestions that determined the vast majority of his unit's battle plans against the Arachen. His superior, the [[Cadre Fireblade]], was eager to test his mettle in the [[Trial by Fire]]. The Trial proved to be a traumatic experience for Shoh, with many of the instructors wishing to finally teach him a lesson. As a result he was put into a near-hopeless live-fire war dome exercise and died in the simulation saving a teammate. Nonetheless when he awoke from the simulation, he was informed that only Shas'la who died in the exercise would be promoted to [[Shas'ui]]. As a result, Shoh could finally pilot a [[Battlesuit]].{{Fn|4d}}

Shoh was initially assigned to a [[Broadside Battlesuit]] team and sent back to the [[Arachen War]] front. During the days he learned the basics of Battlesuit command from the veteran pilot [[Shas'vre]] [[Ob'lotai]]. Shoh saw combat for the first time in a [[Crisis Battlesuit]] during the final action of the war, as [[Commander Dawnstone]] led the warriors of Vior'la into a final push. The extremely bloody battle that followed saw Shoh lose most of his left leg. Nonetheless he performed well in the battle and was promoted to [[Saz'nami]] and led the assault that killed the ruling Arachen council, the Arachen Trinity. The assassination proved to be the final straw that broke the Arachen hold on the Western Veil, and the T'au emerged victorious after the long war. However, tragedy struck during a boarding operation of an Arachen cocoon ship led personally by Dawnstone. During the boarding the T'au team came under attack from a swarm of Arachen Skitterlings, and Shoh sacrificed himself to save Commander Dawnstone.{{Fn|4e}}

Shoh nonetheless soon awoke in a med-bay, where Dawnstone revealed the entire operation had just been a simulation, another Trial by Fire. Shoh's performance during the simulation was so impressive that he was made a disciple of the legendary [[Commander Puretide]] alongside [[Kais]] and [[Shaserra]]. The subsequent training under Puretide on Da'lyth was gruelling and with Kais and Shaserra, he was forced to survive the brutal conditions on [[Mount Kan'ji]].{{Fn|11}} However, under Puretide, Shoh was able to master every aspect of the T'au art of war.{{Fn|4e}} He even made a point of following every presentation made by the [[Earth Caste]] on military equipment that he could, to the point of risking being labelled ''vash'ya'' (between spheres), a state of mind forbidden by the [[Ethereal]]s.{{Fn|7}}

====Command Career====
[[Image:Farsight6.jpg|right|thumb|150px|Farsight in his Battlesuit{{Fn|3b}}]]
Farsight's first and greatest victories were won in the [[Arkunasha War]].{{Fn|2c}} When the T'au colony there was threatened by [[Waaagh! Dok]] under [[Warboss]] [[Toofjaw]], he led the [[Fire Warrior]]s in a masterful defence against a horde many times their own number. Using the immense canyons and gullies criss-crossing the desert to maximum effect, O'Shovah set the [[Ork]] invaders to chase shadows, constantly boxing off and destroying isolated elements wherever they turned at bay, earning him the famous title "farsight" for his actions.{{Fn|2c}} Possibly because of his ongoing battles against Ork forces, [[O'Shovah]] believes that close combat is a vital part of warfare, unlike most other T'au. O'shovah has become the renowned master of the [[Mont'ka]] — aggressive tactic of the T'au forces.{{Fn|6}}

O'shovah was again called upon to defend the [[T'au Empire]] during the incursion of T'au space by the Imperium of Man in what the Imperium called the [[Damocles Crusade]]. He was a major morale booster during the campaign and after nearly being killed in combat against then-[[Assault Marine]]s [[Cato Sicarius]] and [[Jorus Numitor]] pledged revenge against them.{{Fn|7}} After the Imperial forces withdrew he led one of several reclamation fleets to recapture T'au worlds lost to the Imperium, eliminating Imperial deserters and abandoned guardsmen then Farsight continued his explorations well beyond the known T'au space.{{cite this}} 

While a young commander in the Damocles Crusade, the T'au came under attack by Farsight's old enemy, the [[Scar Lords]] Space Marines under acting Chapter Master [[Caelos]]. The Space Marines were able to reach [[Vior'los]], but were [[Battle of Vior'los|ultimately bested and annihilated by Farsight]]. 90% of the Chapter was lost in the failed invasion, though Farsight's faith towards the callous Ethereals was shaken during the war.{{Fn|10}}

====Farsight Expedition====
*{{main|Farsight Expedition}}
Following the Damocles Crusade, [[Aun'va]] recommended an expedition to rekindle hope and spirit in the empire, which had been dwindling since the bloody war against the Imperium. In the ensuing [[Farsight Expedition]], Farsight led an enormous T'au fleet across the Damocles Gulf, going into [[Cryostasis Unit|frozen stasis]] to avoid aging during the long trip.{{Fn|4f}} In the ensuing campaign on the far side of the Damocles Gulf, Farsight was initially beaten at every turn by [[Grog Ironteef]], the [[Ork]] [[Warchief of Alsanta]]. A frustrated O'Shovah nonetheless eventually was able to defeat Grog, and pursued scattered Ork forces to the world of [[Arthas Moloch]]. There, Farsight encountered a terrifying new enemy in the form of [[Chaos Daemon]]s which emerged from a mysterious portal erected on a great dais. During the battle, all of Farsight's accompanying Ethereals were killed by these gruesome creatures, and Farsight was only able to attain victory thanks to discovering a [[Dawn Blade|mysterious blade]] he used to close the portal, though he was briefly overcome by [[Khorne|extreme bloodlust]]{{Fn|12}}. The experience of battling these foes and gazing into their sinister portal rattled O'Shovah, who realised there were far greater threats than the Ethereals would have him know.{{Fn|4g}}

During the conflict, O'Shovah authored the [[Book of the Beast]], which detailed Ork psychology and doctrine.{{Fn|4e}}

====Break with the Ethereals====
{{QuoteBox
|quote = '''''"Each must find their own way. If those in our heartland had witnessed the savageries of the void as have we they would know this. The hand of each of the starfarers is turned against the other; none will join their strength together just to see their ancient enemies prosper. Neither should we.’"'''''
|source = - Commander Farsight{{Fn|2d}}
|}}
After the incident on Arthas Moloch, Farsight returned to his [[Farsight Enclaves|established holdings]] on the far side of the Damocles Gulf. Farsight began to secretly become highly distrustful of the Ethereal Caste, coming to suspect that they may be controlling the T'au by more than simple charisma and wisdom, perhaps employing more sinister methods. In the aftermath of his victory, Farsight came to realise the threat of Chaos and the fact the universe was not the orderly rational place described in T'au philosophy. He also came to the realisation that the Ethereals as a caste knew something of this but had kept it from the T'au.{{Fn|4b}} At first he merely thought their leadership was flawed, that the other castes would be better off seeking harmony with each other without their oversight but increasingly he ruminated on the instances where obedience to the Ethereals had been taken to illogical and foolish extremes. Worse, many of the actions taken under their guidance that had seemed at the time both wise and necessary seemed now deplorable and wrong.{{Fn|4b}} In addition, Farsight began to live far beyond the normal life span of a T'au.{{Fn|4h}}

The inevitable conclusion that the Ethereals' control extended beyond simple logic and loyalty to something darker drove him to sickening panic. Heresies began filling Farsight's mind, and not even he truly knew why. The truth he held could dissolve the Empire and drive the T'au back to the Mont'au, a fate far worse than the Ethereals' rule. Unable to tell anyone this truth but unwilling to pretend or forget, Farsight chose exile over obedience. {{Fn|4b}}

Meanwhile in the T'au Empire, [[Ethereal Supreme]] [[Aun'va]] had discovered the death of the Ethereals on Arthas Moloch and the establishment of a seemingly independent T'au enclave under Farsight. Furious, he ordered O'Shovah to return to the Empire to explain what exactly had transpired. Instead of returning to the T'au Empire, however, Farsight established a series of fortified strongholds along the [[Damocles Gulf]]. Contact was severed between the Farsight Enclaves and the Ethereals and it was finally confirmed that O'Shovah had turned his back on the T'au Empire.{{Fn|4h}}

====Exile====
[[Image:FarsightArt9th.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Farsight{{Fn|9}}]]
Shortly after setting the foundations of the Farsight Enclaves, O'Shovah announced his intention to leave its Elemental Council and go into a self-imposed exile. Judging himself as unworthy and having seen too much during the experience on Arthas Moloch, Farsight left administration of the Enclaves to six of his [[The Eight|closest advisors]] and left to wander the wastelands of [[Vior'los]]. He abandoned his Battlesuit and the Dawnblade, which were both kept to be studied by [[O'Vesa]]. Farsight's decision to exile himself is what ultimately saved him from corruption by the [[Chaos Gods]] [[Khorne]] and [[Tzeentch]], which had begun their ploy to corrupt his soul on Arthas Moloch.{{Fn|12}}

====Tyranid Invasion====
{{Spoiler|source=Farsight: Blade of Truth (Novel)}}
Dwelling in the barren savanna of Vior'los for many years, Farsight sought a natural death but found himself living beyond the natural Tau lifespan and could survive even the harsh environment with little effort. Encountering [[Tyranid]]s in the wastelands of Vior'los, Farsight returned to once again lead the Enclaves in 997.[[M41]] when it was threatened by [[Hive Fleet Kraken]] or [[Hive Fleet Gorgon]]{{Fn|3a}}{{Fn|15b}}{{Fn|Conflicting sources}}. Before taking command, Farsight first made for a war museum that held his Battlesuit and shocked the staff which at first did not believe he was who he claimed to be. Only when he was recognized by [[O'Vesa]] was he allowed to take up his mantle and reunite with [[The Eight]] (at the time seven) to lead the war effort.{{Fn|15c}} During the subsequent campaign against the Tyranids, Farsight was  mortally wounded by the xenos, but was saved by Sub-Commander [[Torchstar]], who had just defected to the Enclaves.{{Fn|15d}} Torchstar would later become the eighth member of The Eight.{{Fn|4i}} 

Once aboard Torchstar's ship, the ''[[Vision of Aun'shi]]'', Farsight and Torchstar were betrayed by Torchstar's bond-mate [[Por'vre Rivertell]] to the [[Ethereal]]s.{{Fn|15e}} Farsight was taken upon the [[T'au Empire|sept space]] ship ''[[Hand of Aun'Va]]'' where he was sentenced to face [[Malk'la]].{{Fn|15f}} Farsight emerged from his Malk'la trial unharmed, and was dragged before [[Aun'Va]].{{Fn|15g}} [[Aun'Va]] decorated his auditorium with images of Farsight's bon-mates, [[Shadowsun]] and [[Shas'O Monat Kais|Kais]], as well as images great victories Farsight had participated in, though with Farsight removed from them.{{Fn|15h}} Aun'Va interrogated Farsight and, following an outburst from Farsight where he described Aun'Va as the "falsest of the aun", Aun'Va challenged Farsight to a duel. As befitting his rank as aun, Aun'Va selected his bodyguard [[Shaz’ai De’tzata]] to fight as his champion. Farsight was forced to fight with a [[Honour Blade]], a weapon he was unfammiliar with, and this caused him to be at such a disadvantage that De’tzata was able to sever Farsight's prosthetic leg. 

As Farsight was about to be killed [[Aun'shi]] revealed himself to the Council, much to Farsight's shock as he beleived Aun'Shi to be long dead. Aun'shi provided Farsight with a spare prosthetic, and announced his intention to fight as Farsight's second in the duel. Aun'Va's second bodyguard joined the battle against Aun'shi and Farsight.{{Fn|15i}} After Farsight and Aun'shi were victorious in their duel Aun'Va reluctantly allowed Farsight to return to [[Vior'los]] with Aun'shi, though he told Aun'shi that his actions defending Farsight would have supreme consequences.{{Fn|15j}}      

During the climax of the campaign, Farsight led eighty Battlesuits to drive the [[Xenos]] back.{{Fn|3a}}{{Fn|15k}}

====Second Agrellan Campaign====
Farsight next appeared during the [[Second Agrellan Campaign]], sending in the full might of his forces alongside his former ally [[Commander Shadowsun]]. Together, the two managed an effective but ultimately doomed resistance against the [[Imperium]]. After the battle, O'Shovah disappeared and made course for [[T'lasa]] to rescue the planet from [[Hive Fleet Leviathan]]. Despite his aid to the Empire the [[Ethereal Council]] ordered Shadowsun to immediately capture him, something she secretly leaked to the exiled commander to try and help him get a headstart.{{Fn|5}}

====Arks of Omen====
After the Agrellan Campaign, Farsight was again frustrated by constant Ork attacks on his Enclaves in the renewed [[War of Dakka]]. The Orks were now led by the infamous [[Nazdreg]], forcing Farsight to attack his world [[Dregrokk]] despite its proximity to Arthas Moloch. While Farsight was successful in seemingly killing Nazdreg (who disappeared into a Warp Portal), [[Chaos]] forces under [[Ughalax]] arrived immediately afterwards in the [[Ark of Omen]] ''[[Unhallowed]]'' seeking a [[Key-Fragment]] on Arthas Moloch.{{Fn|14a}} Farsight was forced to return to the blighted world in a fighting retreat, seeking to have the Orks and Chaos armies destroy one another. However the T'au took heavy losses, and during the fighting [[Brightsword]] was killed while [[Bravestorm]] and [[Ob'lotai]] were badly injured. These proved to frustrate Farsight, who began to apparently succumb to Khornate influence. The situation grew worse when [[Aun'va]] personally rebuked his offer for aid, stating that he and his people were condemned for turning their backs on the Greater Good.{{Fn|14b}}

The war on Arthas Moloch came to its head at the Great Star Dais, where a vengeful Farsight activated its portal with enemy blood and unleashed Daemons upon the Ork and Chaos armies. For a moment, Farsight gave into the fury of Khorne and became an avatar of the Blood God, his Battlesuit transformed into a [[Titan]]-sized axe-wielding monstrosity covered in the skulls of the [[Ethereal]]s he now hated. Farsight began to see himself as an army of one, and that any of his warriors that were too weak to survive on their own deserved their fate. The horror of this thought creeping into his mind drew Farsight back to sanity, and he shook off the corruption. Upon regaining his senses, he immediately ordered a fighting retreat from Arthas Moloch and sought to save his warriors above any other objective. Farsight later returned to the world to examine the Great Dais, and was pleased to find it was inactive and that organic life was returning to the world.{{Fn|14c}}

===Assets===
====Personal Wargear====
Farsight pilots an [[Crisis Battlesuit_#XV86 Coldstar Battlesuit|XV86 Battlesuit]] with a number of improvements, including an advanced [[XV9|Hazard Suite]] developed during the [[Arkunasha War]] and armed with a [[Plasma Rifle]].{{Fn|7}} Since his experience on [[ Arthas Moloch]], he wields the mysterious [[Dawn Blade]] of unknown origin.{{Fn|4g}}

====The Eight====
*{{main|The Eight}}
Farsight does not lead a conventional bodyguard; instead he draws from a band of warriors known as [[The Eight]]. The eight is notable for having a number of [[Shas'o]] in its ranks, rather than the normal [[Shas'vre]] of a bodyguard. It also includes [[Ob'lotai 9-0|an AI controlling a Broadside suit]], [[Bravestorm|a T'au warrior sealed in his suite as a life support aide]] in the fashion of an Imperial [[Dreadnought]] and even [[O'Vesa|a member of the Earth caste]] piloting a modified [[Riptide]].{{Fn|4i}}

